answer:  The reflection matrix over a line passing through the origin can be written as:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta & -\cos \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta & \sin \theta \\
-\cos \theta & \sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Given the matrix, we can see that \(\cos \theta = -\frac{2}{3}\), \(\sin \theta = -\frac{1}{3}\), and \(\theta = \arccos \left(-\frac{2}{3}\right)\).

The direction vector of the line \(\ell\) is perpendicular to the line passing through the origin. The direction vector can be found using the angle \(\theta\). The direction vector is given by:

\[
\begin{pmatrix} \cos \theta \\ \sin \theta \\ -\cos \theta \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{2}{3} \\ -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the direction vector of line \(\ell\) is \(\boxed{(2, -1, 2)}\).
